steps.body:
Most problems give a count first, then that many values. Read `n`, then loop `n` times.

```cpp
int n = 0;
std::cin >> n;
std::vector<int> v;
for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    int x = 0;
    std::cin >> x;
    v.push_back(x);
}
```

When you know `n` up front you can also size the vector first and read straight into each slot:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> n;
    {{code}}
    for (int x : v) {
        std::cout << x << ' ';
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```stdin
4
3 8 1 6
```

```cpp
std::vector<int> v(n);                 // n zeros on the heap
for (std::size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i) {
    std::cin >> v[i];
}
```

Do not mix the two: `std::vector<int> v(n)` followed by `push_back` gives you `n` zeros plus `n` values. The sized form allocates the heap buffer once, while `push_back` may reallocate as the vector grows.

Keep reading and processing separate. Read everything into the vector first, then compute on it with loops or `<algorithm>` functions. That makes the logic easy to test and reuse.

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <numeric>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> n;
    std::vector<int> values;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int x = 0;
        std::cin >> x;
        values.push_back(x);
    }

    const int sum = std::accumulate(values.begin(), values.end(), 0);
    const int smallest = *std::min_element(values.begin(), values.end());
    const int largest = *std::max_element(values.begin(), values.end());
    std::cout << "sum " << sum << '\n';
    std::cout << "min " << smallest << '\n';
    std::cout << "max " << largest << '\n';
    return 0;
}

