steps.body:
Sometimes there is no count: you read until the input runs out. Put the read itself in the loop condition.

```cpp
int x = 0;
while (std::cin >> x) {
    // use x
}
```

`std::cin >> x` returns `std::cin`, and a stream converts to `true` while it is healthy. When a read fails, because the input ended or the next token is not a number, the stream converts to `false` and the loop stops. The failed read does not give you a new value, so the body never sees it.

This works for any type: `while (std::cin >> word)` reads words until the end, across as many lines as there are.

> Gotcha: do not write `while (!std::cin.eof())` and read inside. `eof()` only turns true after a read has tried to go past the end, so with a trailing newline that loop runs one extra time with a stale value.

Speed tip: for very large inputs, put `std::ios::sync_with_stdio(false);` as the first line of `main`. It stops C++ streams from syncing with C's `scanf` and `printf`, which makes `std::cin` much faster. After that, do not mix the two styles.

