steps.body:
`>>` stops at a space, so it cannot read `Grace Hopper` as one value. `std::getline(std::cin, line)` from `<string>` reads everything up to the end of the line, spaces included, and throws away the newline.

The trap is mixing the two. `std::cin >> n` stops right before the newline after the number and leaves it in the input. A `getline` after it reads that leftover newline and returns an empty line.

```cpp
int n = 0;
std::cin >> n >> std::ws;   // read n, then skip the leftover newline
std::string line;
std::getline(std::cin, line);
```

`std::ws` skips all whitespace up to the next real character. Use it whenever a `getline` follows a `>>`. Note that it also skips leading spaces and blank lines, so use it only where those do not matter.

To read every remaining line, put `getline` in the loop condition. Like `>>`, it returns the stream, which turns `false` once no lines are left. A last line without a final newline is still read:

```cpp
std::string line;
while (std::getline(std::cin, line)) {
    std::cout << line.size() << '\n';
}
```

example.code:
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::size_t minLength = 0;
    std::cin >> minLength >> std::ws;
    std::string name;
    while (std::getline(std::cin, name)) {
        if (name.size() >= minLength) {
            std::cout << name << " (" << name.size() << ")\n";
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

